{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgheDLpVt6iAfSvyrtcge","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For this high-spin octahedral d⁷ complex, **Dq ≈ 979 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 9 790 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 853 cm⁻¹**. I worked this out by hand from the standard band equations; I did not use the fitting tools.\n\n**Band assignment** (ground state ⁴T₁g(F))\n- ν₁ = 8 620 cm⁻¹: ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g(F)\n- ν₂ = 18 410 cm⁻¹: ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴A₂g(F), the two-electron jump t₂g⁵e_g² → t₂g³e_g⁴\n- ν₃ = 20 250 cm⁻¹: ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₁g(P)\n\n**Band equations**\n\nThe two ⁴T₁g states mix. Diagonalising their 2×2 matrix (F-term energy set to zero) gives:\n- R = √(225B² + 180DqB + 100Dq²)\n- E[⁴T₁g(F)] = 7.5B − 3Dq − R/2 and E[⁴T₁g(P)] = 7.5B − 3Dq + R/2\n- E(⁴T₂g) = +2Dq and E(⁴A₂g) = +12Dq\n\nSo:\n- ν₁ = 5Dq − 7.5B + R/2\n- ν₂ = 15Dq − 7.5B + R/2, which means ν₂ − ν₁ = 10Dq exactly\n- ν₃ = R\n\n**Solving**\n1. 10Dq = ν₂ − ν₁ = 18 410 − 8 620 = 9 790 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 979 cm⁻¹**.\n2. Set R = ν₃ = 20 250 cm⁻¹ in the ν₁ equation: 8 620 = 4 895 − 7.5B + 10 125. This gives 7.5B = 6 400, so **B ≈ 853 cm⁻¹**.\n3. Check: with Dq = 979 and B = 853, R = √(225·853² + 180·979·853 + 100·979²) ≈ √(4.099 × 10⁸) ≈ 20 246 cm⁻¹. This matches the observed 20 250 cm⁻¹, so the assignment holds together.\n\n**Other assignment and Racah C**\n- If the 18 410 and 20 250 cm⁻¹ bands are swapped, the three bands no longer fit consistently (I did not show this working).\n- Racah C cannot be found from these data. Spin-allowed quartet-to-quartet energies depend only on Dq and B; C would need spin-forbidden (doublet) bands.\n\n{\"Dq\": 979, \"B\": 853, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Three spin-allowed quartet bands fit exactly with 10Dq = ν2 − ν1 and ν3 = R; C needs spin-forbidden bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7985,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_c